  { "ref": "Misc Q19", "book_key": "-2\\sqrt{1-x}+\\cos^{-1}\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{x-x^2}+C", "our_answer": "-2\\sqrt{1-x}-\\sin^{-1}\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{x-x^2}+C", "verdict": "AGREE", "note": "Differ only by the constant \\pi/2: \\cos^{-1}\\sqrt{x} = \\tfrac{\\pi}{2}-\\sin^{-1}\\sqrt{x}, so key = ours + \\pi/2, absorbed into C. Re-derived: (1-\\sqrt{x})/\\sqrt{1-x} = 1/\\sqrt{1-x} - \\sqrt{x}/\\sqrt{1-x}; first term \\to -2\\sqrt{1-x}, second (x=\\sin^2\\theta) \\to \\sin^{-1}\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{x-x^2}, giving -2\\sqrt{1-x}-\\sin^{-1}\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{x-x^2}+C. Equivalent." },
